          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A note says hypertension and chronic kidney disease. Does the classification assume a relationship between them, and what changes if it does?",
          "answer": "**Yes, the classification presumes a link.** The sources say that when hypertension and chronic kidney disease (CKD) are listed together, the classification treats them as related and pairs them without needing the note to say so. This is part of a general rule: where the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification reads it as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" unless the record says otherwise. [coding-type-2-diabetes-e11; coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions]\n\n**What the presumption changes:** Hypertension with CKD is coded to combination code I12, with a second code for the CKD stage. If heart disease is also present, the combination is I13. Without the presumption, hypertension would stand alone as I10, which the sources say applies only when hypertension has no organ involvement. [coding-hypertension-i10]\n\n**If the documentation says the two are unrelated:** The sources state that the presumption is overridden when the record says the conditions are unrelated. They do not spell out the exact codes assigned in that case, so I can't tell you from these materials which codes would replace I12. Check the official tabular list and coding guidelines, or ask a certified coder.\n\n**A contrast with heart disease:** The presumption is specific to kidney disease. For heart disease, the two are coded separately unless the record states the heart condition is hypertensive or due to the hypertension. [coding-hypertension-i10]\n\nThis explains how the classification reads a note. It does not tell you what your own note means or what codes were actually assigned to it. For that, ask the clinician or the coding staff who handled the record.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
